nassigned variable, and neither can\n *     shrink, so anything above the incumbent is dead;\n *   - neither: deepen on the total, which finds a cheap answer hiding in a huge\n *     null space, and fall back to improving the incumbent if time runs out.\n *\n * Only the `rank` pivot counters have to be tracked while searching: every\n * other counter's value is simply the number of presses assigned to it.\n *\n * A machine that is dense, wide and high modulus at once (say 30 counters and\n * 40 buttons mod 97) is a shortest vector problem with no known fast exact\n * answer; there the budget runs out and the best solution found is returned.\n */","originalFile":"/** Milliseconds the search may spend before returning the best it has found. */\nconst SEARCH_BUDGET_MS = 3000;\n\n/** Above this many candidate assignments, deepening beats a plain sweep. */\nconst FULL_SWEEP_LIMIT = 5e6;\n\n/**\n * Counter variant: button `j` pressed `x[j]` times adds `x[j]` to every counter\n * it touches, so the puzzle is `A x = lights (mod p)` with `x` in `0..p-1`.\n *\n * Row reducing mod p gives one particular solution plus a null space of\n * dimension `f`; every solution is `x0 + sum(t_k * v_k)` for free choices of\n * `t`. Total presses is not linear in `t` (every counter wraps), so we search\n * the `t` space rather than solve for it, with two levers:\n *\n *   - a bound: a partial assignment has already committed `sum(t_k)` plus every\n *     pivot counter that no longer depends on an unassigned variable, and\n *     neither can shrink, so anything above the incumbent is dead;\n *   - iterative deepening on the total, for when the null space is far too big\n *     to sweep but the answer is cheap.\n *\n * Only the `rank` pivot counters have to be tracked while searching: every\n * other counter's value is simply the number of presses assigned to it.\n */\nexport function solveCounter(\n  lights: number[],\n  buttons: number[][],\n  modulus: number,\n): number[] | null {\n  const p = modulus;\n  if (!Number.isInteger(p) || p < 1) return null;\n\n  const n = lights.length;\n  const m = buttons.length;\n  const stride = m + 1;\n\n  const mat = new Int32Array(n * stride);\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    mat[i * stride + m] = (((lights[i] ?? 0) % p) + p) % p;\n  }\n  for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {\n    const touched = buttons[j] ?? [];\n    for (let k = 0; k < touched.length; k++) {\n      const counter = touched[k];\n      // Counters outside the target carry no constraint; a repeated index in\n      // one button increments that counter once per occurrence.\n      if (counter >= 0 && counter < n) {\n        mat[counter * stride + j] = (mat[counter * stride + j] + 1) % p;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  const pivotCols: number[] = [];\n  let rank = 0;\n  for (let col = 0; col < m && rank < n; col++) {\n    let sel = -1;\n    for (let i = rank; i < n; i++) {\n      if (mat[i * stride + col] !== 0) {\n        sel = i;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    if (sel < 0) continue;\n    if (sel !== rank) {\n      for (let c = 0; c < stride; c++) {\n        const tmp = mat[rank * stride + c];\n        mat[rank * stride + c] = mat[sel * stride + c];\n        mat[sel * stride + c] = tmp;\n      }\n    }\n    const inv = modInverse(mat[rank * stride + col], p);\n    for (let c = col; c < stride; c++) {\n      mat[rank * stride + c] = (mat[rank * stride + c] * inv) % p;\n    }\n    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n      const factor = mat[i * stride + col];\n      if (i === rank || factor === 0) continue;\n      for (let c = col; c < stride; c++) {\n        const v = (mat[i * stride + c] - factor * mat[rank * stride + c]) % p;\n        mat[i * stride + c] = v < 0 ? v + p : v;\n      }\n    }\n    pivotCols.push(col);\n    rank++;\n  }\n\n  // Rows past the rank read 0 = rhs, so a non-zero right hand side is a\n  // constraint no press vector can satisfy.\n  for (let i = rank; i < n; i++) {\n    if (mat[i * stride + m] !== 0) return null;\n  }\n\n  const isPivot = new Uint8Array(m);\n  for (const col of pivotCols) isPivot[col] = 1;\n  const unordered: number[] = [];\n  for (let col = 0; col < m; col++) if (!isPivot[col]) unordered.push(col);\n  const free = unordered.length;\n\n  // Few enough independent counters to enumerate every reachable configuration?\n  // Then walking the configurations beats searching the null space, and it\n  // stays exact no matter how many buttons there are.\n  const states = p ** rank;\n  const assignments = p ** free;\n  if (\n    (m + 1) * states <= 6e6 &&\n    m * states * (rank + 4) <= 3e8 &&\n    (assignments > FULL_SWEEP_LIMIT || m * states * (rank + 4) < assignments * (rank + 2))\n  ) {\n    return solveByStates(mat, stride, rank, m, p, states);\n  }\n\n  const freeCols = orderFreeColumns(mat, stride, rank, unordered);\n\n  // Pivot counters with every free variable at zero.\n  const rootPivots = new Int32Array(rank);\n  for (let i = 0; i < rank; i++) rootPivots[i] = mat[i * stride + m];\n\n  // What one press of free button k does to the pivot counters.\n  const pivotBasis: Int32Array[] = [];\n  for (let k = 0; k < free; k++) {\n    const vec = new Int32Array(rank);\n    for (let i = 0; i < rank; i++) vec[i] = (p - mat[i * stride + freeCols[k]]) % p;\n    pivotBasis.push(vec);\n  }\n\n  let best: Int32Array | null = null;\n  let bestCost = Infinity;\n\n  /** Expands a compact state into a full press vector and keeps it if it wins. */\n  const record = (pivots: Int32Array, presses: Int32Array, cost: number): void => {\n    if (cost > bestCost) return;\n    const candidate = new Int32Array(m);\n    for (let i = 0; i < rank; i++) candidate[pivotCols[i]] = pivots[i];\n    for (let k = 0; k < free; k++) candidate[freeCols[k]] = presses[k];\n    if (cost < bestCost || lexLess(candidate, best!)) {\n      best = candidate;\n      bestCost = cost;\n    }\n  };\n\n  const trail = new Int32Array(free);\n  record(rootPivots, trail, sumOf(rootPivots));\n\n  // A greedy pass first: any decent solution tightens the bound the search\n  // prunes with, which is worth far more than the pass costs.\n  if (free > 0) coordinateDescent(rootPivots, pivotBasis, p, record);\n\n  // Which free variable each pivot counter stops depending on. Once past it\n  // that counter is final and can be charged against the bound.\n  const settledAt: number[][] = [];\n  for (let k = 0; k < free; k++) settledAt.push([]);\n  let baseSettled = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < rank; i++) {\n    let last = -1;\n    for (let k = 0; k < free; k++) {\n      if (mat[i * stride + freeCols[k]] !== 0) last = k;\n    }\n    if (last < 0) baseSettled += rootPivots[i];\n    else settledAt[last].push(i);\n  }\n\n  // One scratch state per depth, so descending never has to undo arithmetic.\n  const levels: Int32Array[] = [rootPivots.slice()];\n  for (let k = 0; k < free; k++) levels.push(new Int32Array(rank));\n\n  const startedAt = Date.now();\n  let deadline = startedAt + SEARCH_BUDGET_MS;\n  let countdown = 4096;\n  let aborted = false;\n  /** Ceiling on the total for this pass; the incumbent tightens it further. */\n  let cap = Infinity;\n  let reachedLeaf = false;\n\n  const search = (depth: number, state: Int32Array, pressed: number, settled: number): void => {\n    if (--countdown <= 0) {\n      countdown = 4096;\n      if (Date.now() > deadline) {\n        aborted = true;\n        return;\n      }\n    }\n    if (depth === free) {\n      reachedLeaf = true;\n      record(state, trail, pressed + settled);\n      return;\n    }\n    const dst = levels[depth + 1];\n    const vec = pivotBasis[depth];\n    const settling = settledAt[depth];\n    for (let v = 0; v < p; v++) {\n      const limit = cap < bestCost ? cap : bestCost;\n      // Presses and settled counters only ever get added to, so this is a valid\n      // lower bound for the whole subtree. Equal totals stay in play because\n      // they can still win on the tie break.\n      if (pressed + v + settled > limit) break;\n      // Not pressin